From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Dylan Ironside <dylan@dylanironside.io>
Date: Wed, 18 Nov 2026 17:30:04 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] drm/asahi: g16: Optionally run independent compute on
 more firmware queues

The firmware runs the commands of one compute queue one at a time:
StartCompute sets the queue's busy bit and the wait op parks the queue
until it clears, whatever the depth. Different queues run at once. macOS
gives one client two to four compute queues on one channel and rotates
its commands over them; all of ours go to one queue.

Add g16_compute_queues (default 1, latched at the engine start). With 2
to 4, every extra queue is made as the first one is (queue info, state,
ring, job list, notifier page, threshold and stamps) under the shared
scheduling context. It registers with its first submit record on an
idle engine, under queue id 16, 17 or 18.

A compute command goes to the queue after its predecessor's only when
every barrier, historical prior and in-fence it names has retired, so it
names no running command; a command naming running work waits for it to
retire. With g16_job_ahead or g16_same_queue_nowait every command stays
on queue 0. More than one queue needs g16_compute_shared_pool 2 and
the compute sequence pool off.

Flights still retire in publication order. Each is proved on its own
queue's stamp, cursors and notifier stamp, which no other queue writes,
and on its own unit's timestamp pair. The shared completion count of
mode 2 stays a necessary term but never proves a flight alone, because
a later command on another queue may finish first. Recovery snapshots
and affected-work entries are taken per queue as well.

Assisted-by: LLM
